  the compressor experience the same air  pressure and speed through each cycle  but if we move the engines tie it  against the back of the plane the  engines have to ingest the boundary  layer airflow which is the slow-moving  layer of air that builds up on the  surface of the plane this type of engine  is called a boundary layer ingestion  engine and it has been a topic of great  interest for NASA and other aerospace  companies because it reduces the loss of  kinetic energy of the aircraft greatly  in a normal plane this boundary layer of  slow-moving air simply rolls off the  back of the plane and mixes with the  fast-moving air this causes vortices and  a low-pressure zone behind the plane  which creates drag the idea behind the  boundary layer ingesting engines is that  they take the slow-moving air and speed  it up and thus eliminate some of the  drag it's a nice idea that it's fair for  being ready the first problem we face is  that non-uniform air entering the  engines the air entering the engines  furthest from the fuselage of the plane  is moving faster than the air entering  from near the surface this creates a  discontinuity of stress as discussed  before in my dream liner video cycling  through high and low stresses is very  bad for any part as a result in fatigue  of the parish and when your part is  rotating true those high and low  stresses a few tells in times per minute  your part isn't going to last very long  and that's just problem number one the  next big problem is stalled air flow  normally moves the uniformly through a  jet engine but when it's distorted as it  enters the engine there's a high risk of  compressor store compressor stall works  similarly to stall on a wing where the  speed and angle of attack of the wing  can result in flow separation behind the  wing this prevents the wing from  generating lift and the stall occurs  non-uniform turbulent air makes it far  more likely to occur when this happens  in a compressor it can lead to 